#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073415 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073793 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "940366ef-28e8-5827-b52d-a8b500dbb2a1" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Fraction by a Whole Number: Result Is a Mixed Number"}] :question/id #uuid "3deda9a1-7edd-5b1e-9f39-5c69644f30ab" :question/math-academy-id "e-10542" :question/problem "Greg stacked $5$ cardboard boxes on top of each other. How tall was his pile if each box is $\\dfrac{3}{4}\\,\\textrm{ft}$ high?" :question/worked-solution "To find out the height of the pile of boxes, we have to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nby $5$.\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5 \\times \\dfrac34 = \\dfrac{5\\times 3}{4} = \\dfrac{15}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {15}{4}\n$$\n\nto a mixed number:\n\n$$\n15 \\div 4 = 3\\,\\textrm{R}\\,3 = 3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nSo, Greg's stack of boxes was\n\n$$\n3\\,\\dfrac{3}{4}\\,\\textrm{ft}\n$$\n\nhigh."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073416 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073794 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b58f3a78-53b1-577f-8907-d53a00186686" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whole Number Bounds of a Multiplication Problem"}] :question/id #uuid "9fb671f7-6d04-5863-9683-3b2c3753df73" :question/math-academy-id "e-10543" :question/problem "Mr. Stewart spends $\\dfrac56$ hours watching an episode of his favorite TV show. If $5$ episodes are played every week, find the total time he spends watching the show every week. Between what two whole numbers does your answer lie?" :question/worked-solution "To find out how long Mr. Stewart watches the show each week, we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nby $5$.\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n5\\times\\dfrac56 = \\dfrac{5\\times 5}{6} = \\dfrac{25}6\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {25}{6}\n$$\n\nto a mixed number:\n\n$$\n25 \\div 6 = 4\\,\\textrm{R}\\,1 = 4\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, Mr. Stewart spends a total of\n\n$$\n4\\,\\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nhours watching the show, which lies between $4$ and $5$ hours."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073538 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8b003cb-5504-549c-8da4-fdc91242c8af" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Fraction by a Whole Number: Result Is a Whole Number"}] :question/id #uuid "737b03c4-95f0-5a89-a428-e051b9efee6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1863" :question/problem "Larry filled his water bottle with $12\\, \\mathrm{oz}$ of drinking water. If he drank $\\dfrac34$ of the bottle's contents after exercising, how much water did Larry drink?" :question/worked-solution "To find out how much water Larry drank, we need to multiply $12$ by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTo multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac34 \\times 12 = \\dfrac{3\\times 12}{4} = \\dfrac{36}4\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{36}{4} = 36 \\div 4=9\n$$\n\nSo, Larry drank $9\\,\\textrm{oz}$ of water."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8b003cb-5504-549c-8da4-fdc91242c8af" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying a Fraction by a Whole Number: Result Is a Whole Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb6a7026-11d7-5d17-a436-95fc30cb0865" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072825} {:answer/id #uuid "ced9ff27-b43c-538f-b257-198763a2e9e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3lb" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186072826} {:answer/id #uuid "d1a06